**Observation.** If A is related to B, B is often related to A, and chains can loop (A to B to C to A). So the data is a **directed graph**, not a tree. Printing "all related products" recursively without protection would loop forever. Related products can also number in the thousands, so state whether you want the whole reachable graph or limit the depth.

**Class design:**

```java
class Product {
  final String url;
  final String name;
  final double price;
  final List<String> relatedUrls;   // adjacency list: urls, not objects
}
class ProductService {                // wraps the utility functions
  Product fetch(String url) {
    return new Product(url, getProductName(url), getProductPrice(url),
                       getRelatedProducts(url));
  }
}
```
Keep a `Map<String, Product> cache` keyed by URL (or product id). Each URL is fetched at most once, which also acts as the visited set.

**Traversal (BFS, with visited set):**

```java
void printAll(String start) {
  Set<String> visited = new HashSet<>(Set.of(start));
  Deque<String> q = new ArrayDeque<>(List.of(start));
  while (!q.isEmpty()) {
    Product p = service.fetch(q.poll());
    System.out.println(p.name + " - " + p.price);
    for (String r : p.relatedUrls)
      if (visited.add(r)) q.add(r);
  }
}
```
`visited.add` returns false if the URL has been seen, so every product is processed once. Complexity: O(V + E) plus V x 3 remote calls.

**DFS vs BFS.** DFS (recursive) mirrors the structure and prints the product followed by its related items, but may recurse deeply; BFS prints nearest related products first and is easy to cap with a depth limit ("related to depth 2").

**Improvements:** fetch products in parallel (an executor) since calls are I/O bound; batch calls if the API supports it; normalise the key (strip tracking parameters from the URL); cap depth or count; handle failures per product and keep going; if the structure is a tree by design, say so and drop the visited set.
